{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdgTC7GnKwQCPgX4y7yt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EMxeMgHW6YGqGmZ31LokNp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_0\ncat << 'EOF' > hypothesis.md\nPeople do not judge the two sequences independently: they read the left sequence first and its impression becomes an anchor, so each person weighs the first-read sequence's impression of randomness by a personal anchoring weight (more, or less, than the second sequence's), drawn from a population. The choice therefore depends not only on which sequence looks more random but on how random the pair looks overall relative to an ideal-looking sequence — when both look clearly non-random, a person who over-weights the first sequence's flaws turns to the right one, and when both look nearly ideal they stay with the left one — so the same sequence is chosen at different rates beside different partners, and people's left-choice rates and agreement with the majority differ in a pair-dependent way. Each sequence's impression is the current best judgement (personal ideal switching rate and sensitivity with lenience toward over-alternation, personal weights on imbalance, the longest streak and complement symmetry, shared weights on the final streak, mirror symmetry, run-length variety and the most lopsided stretch, and a personal guessing rate).\nEOF\necho personal_first_read_anchoring > model_name.txt\npython3 - << 'EOF'\nsrc = open('../../models/personal_complement_symmetry_aversion.py').read()\ndoc_end = src.index('\"\"\"', 3) + 3\ndoc = '''\"\"\"Personal first-read anchoring.\n\nPeople read the left sequence first and its impression of randomness becomes\nan anchor: each person weighs the first-read sequence's impression by a\npersonal anchoring weight (1 + alpha) and the second's by (1 - alpha), alpha\ndrawn from a population. The comparison therefore depends on how random the\npair looks overall (relative to an ideal-looking sequence, score 0), not only\non the difference, so the same sequence is judged differently beside a\ndifferent partner. Each sequence's impression is the incumbent's\n(personal_complement_symmetry_aversion) judgement.\n\"\"\"'''\nsrc = doc + src[doc_end:]\nold = \"    p_engaged = pm.math.sigmoid(score_a - score_b)\\n\"\nnew = '''    # Personal anchoring on the first-read (left) sequence's impression.\n    mu_alpha = pm.Normal(\"mu_alpha\", mu=0.0, sigma=0.3)\n    sigma_alpha = pm.HalfNormal(\"sigma_alpha\", sigma=0.3)\n    z_alpha = pm.Normal(\"z_alpha\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0, shape=MAX_PARTICIPANTS)\n    alpha = mu_alpha + sigma_alpha * z_alpha\n    a_i = alpha[participant_id]\n    p_engaged = pm.math.sigmoid((1.0 + a_i) * score_a - (1.0 - a_i) * score_b)\n'''\nassert old in src\nsrc = src.replace(old, new)\nopen('candidate.py','w').write(src)\nEOF\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run1/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate \\\n    --candidate-dir /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_0 \\\n    --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5894,"cache_read_input_tokens":78106,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5894,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cee3da27-9fd3-4906-ab1b-8f04833e24b5","uuid":"df53ae