{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdhJ8jUqgZCNNncH4Eiu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate and it passes the self-check (`OK`), with one warning: on the short smoke fit, PSIS-LOO looked unreliable. I haven't checked whether it is new enough to pass the novelty gate, or whether it converges.\n\n**Model name:** `personal_first_read_anchoring`\n\n**Hypothesis:** People read the left sequence first, and its impression of randomness becomes an anchor. Each person weighs the first sequence's impression by their own anchoring weight, which can be more or less than the weight on the second sequence and is drawn from a population. So the choice depends on how random the pair looks overall, not just on which one looks more random:\n- **When both look clearly non-random,** someone who dwells on the first sequence's flaws tends to pick the right one.\n- **When both look close to ideal,** they tend to stay with the left one.\n\nThe same sequence is therefore chosen at different rates beside different partners. This matches the brief's call for a comparison effect. It also targets the critique's main gap (people differ more than predicted in how often they agree with the majority, which survived FDR) and its side-bias gap. Unlike a fixed personal button bias, the lean to one side changes from pair to pair.\n\n**Implementation:** It takes the incumbent `personal_complement_symmetry_aversion` and changes one thing: the comparison becomes `(1 + αᵢ)·score_left − (1 − αᵢ)·score_right`, with a personal, non-centred αᵢ (priors: μ ~ N(0, 0.3), σ ~ HalfNormal(0.3)). How each sequence is scored, and the personal guessing rate, are the incumbent's, unchanged.\n\n**Self-check:** It loads, passes the import allowlist, has a finite log-probability, honours the data contract, and gave a finite ELPD-LOO of −3467.0 (the incumbent's is −3465.7). The one-chain smoke fit (100 tune, 100 draws) took 209 s and gave no R-hat. 10% of trials had Pareto k > 0.5, above the 1% tolerance. That may just be the very short fit, but it also may not be. If the full fit still flags it, the model can still be admitted, but it won't count as trusted for pruning or as the exported winner.\n\n**Novelty risk:** An earlier shared-weight version, `first_read_anchor_weighting`, was rejected as a near-duplicate, probably because its weight fitted close to zero. This version makes the weight personal, but if the population of weights also centres near zero it could be rejected the same way.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_0/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2322,"cache_read_input_tokens":86127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2322,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cee3da27-9fd3-4906-ab1b-8f04833e24b5","uuid":"df06a83a-3adf-4938-9477-6793d8452db8","timestamp":"2026-10-02T16:05:26.395Z","request_id":"req_011CfdhJ8JwJV3zNUVcSt25S"}
